The selected family is now concrete: a native \(\ell=2\) mixed-cluster editing instance represented as a bipartite Cayley graph. The planted certificate is sampled first as “offset orbits” of edge additions/deletions; applying it restores a binary subspace, whose cosets are exactly complete bipartite components. This is composition by identities, not a solve. I’m encoding it as Track B because exhaustive basis decoding is efficient on a machine, while the short route is to recognize XOR-closure.
